WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.030
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.800
NANCY KANWISHER: In a very deep
sense, the brain is who we are.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.610
And so by looking at
what it's made up of,

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.830
what it's pieces are and
what each piece does,

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.960
I think that's
starting to sketch

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.322
for us a picture of the mind.

00:00:17.322 --> 00:00:18.780
SARAH HANSEN: Today
on Chalk Radio,

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.620
we're exploring our most
critical biological asset,

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.120
our brain.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.490
I'm your host, Sarah Hansen.

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.380
My guest this week
is a scientist

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:32.478
and MIT Professor who studies
the architecture of the brain.

00:00:32.478 --> 00:00:34.020
NANCY KANWISHER:
I'm Nancy Kanwisher,

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:37.260
and I'm an investigator in the
McGovern Institute for Brain

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.900
Research and a member of
the Department of Brain

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.580
and Cognitive Sciences.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.700
And I study the human brain
and how it's organized

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.490
at a very macro scale level.

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:51.570
I'm interested in big parts
of the brain and what they do.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:53.820
SARAH HANSEN: In addition
to being an investigator,

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.040
Nancy Kanwisher is
a founding member

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.200
of the McGovern
Institute, which is

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.810
a community of neuroscientists
who are working

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.240
to better understand the brain.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:06.630
I was curious how Nancy got
into her field of research

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.180
and the advancements that
have developed over the years

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.760
since she's been working in it.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.370
NANCY KANWISHER: When I
started in cognitive psychology

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:17.820
the methods we had available
were pretty rudimentary.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.640
I measured reaction
time and accuracy

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.610
when people did simple
tasks looking at pictures

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.440
and understanding sentences.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:29.460
And from that very
sparse low-tech data

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:32.040
of people's responses,
behavioral responses

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:36.360
in different tasks, we tried
to infer the algorithms running

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:37.500
in their heads.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.310
And that's kind of like trying
to figure out how a car works

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.470
just by driving it around.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.593
And you could
discover some stuff.

00:01:45.593 --> 00:01:47.760
You could probably figure
out that there's something

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.247
like gearshifts just by
studying its behavior.

00:01:51.247 --> 00:01:53.580
You could figure out a bunch
of things about car engines

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.542
just by driving them around.

00:01:55.542 --> 00:01:57.750
I mean, you could figure
out even more once you study

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.520
the different ways
that cars break down,

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:01.350
like OK, sometimes
they have this problem

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:02.892
and sometimes they
have that problem,

00:02:02.892 --> 00:02:04.470
and then from that
you could infer,

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:07.020
even if you didn't
know anything before,

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:09.783
what the different pieces
of a car engine might do.

00:02:09.783 --> 00:02:11.700
So for a long time,
that's what our field had,

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.940
is basically driving brains
around and studying what

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.770
happens when they break down.

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:20.190
But then more recently
these amazing methods

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.890
came along that enabled
us to look inside.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.920
And so in my first
year in graduate school

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.990
the first non-invasive study
of function in the human brain

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.120
was published on the
cover of Science.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.940
And here was this
unbelievably blurry picture,

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:39.180
but nevertheless a
picture of activity

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.510
in the back of the head
where visual cortex resides.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.820
And that picture
just blew my mind.

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:49.770
And I wrote to every brain
imaging lab in the world,

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:52.020
there were only
about 5 back then,

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.660
and I said I have these
ideas for experiments,

00:02:54.660 --> 00:03:01.230
and I sent them my
proposals, and crickets.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:04.110
And so I kept at it,
and it was, literally,

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:07.530
10 years before I got to do
a brain imaging experiment.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.000
Spending a lot of
that time beating

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.530
on the doors trying to get
access to the machines,

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:13.890
but the machines were
big and expensive.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:18.030
They were controlled mostly by
MDs, who to be blunt about it,

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.760
didn't really know what
psychological questions to ask

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.060
because that's not their field.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.120
We psychologists are the
ones with the questions.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.340
We were the ones who
were studying minds

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.840
and who knew what to
look for in brains.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:31.500
But we psychologists didn't get
to use the big fancy machines

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.630
for another decade.

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:37.560
And then it's been really
quite a party since then.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.120
SARAH HANSEN: Nancy
explained to me

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.820
that our most basic
understanding of the brain

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:46.320
has been known for a long period
of time, 100 years or more.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.650
And interestingly, some of the
most astounding discoveries

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:52.620
have come not from
studying the healthy brain

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.770
but by studying damaged ones.

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:58.140
More recently, with
new technologies

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:02.670
such as functional MRI, which
is a specific type of MRI that

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:05.490
scans for metabolic
function in the brain,

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:09.090
we've been able to expand and
grow our knowledge in ways that

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:12.720
provide information that's far
more specific than we've ever

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.710
had before.

00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:17.240
NANCY KANWISHER:
For a couple years

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:21.130
we've had the idea that
regions in the left side

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:24.040
of the lateral, temporal,
and frontal lobes

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:26.560
are involved in
processing language.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.390
And we've known
that because people

00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:31.600
who have damage to those regions
sometimes lose their language

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:32.980
abilities.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:35.200
And similarly, we
knew that somewhere

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:39.220
in the back of the right
hemisphere near the bottom

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:41.590
there was some kind
of function that was

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:44.360
involved in face recognition.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:46.420
And again, we knew that
from the small number

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:49.750
of people who had damage
to that part of the brain

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:52.810
and lost their ability
to recognize faces,

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:56.080
sometimes without losing their
ability to recognize objects,

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:59.080
or words, or scenes, and
that was strongly suggestive,

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:01.570
that, hey, maybe there's
a special bit of the brain

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:03.490
for face recognition.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:06.400
That kind of stuff has
been known for a long time,

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:08.380
but that was it, right?

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:09.940
And then functional
MRI came along

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:13.000
and now we have dozens
of regions of cortex

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:14.890
where we have a pretty
good idea of what

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:17.590
each of those little
patches of cortex does.

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:20.800
And many of those regions do
a very, very specific things

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:23.860
like just thinking about what
other people are thinking,

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:30.670
or just understanding language,
or just face recognition.

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:32.980
SARAH HANSEN:
Facial recognition.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:36.280
That's a topic that gets
talked about a lot at my house,

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:40.000
mostly because my spouse
seems to struggle with it.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.372
I was curious if
this was typical.

00:05:43.372 --> 00:05:45.080
NANCY KANWISHER: Never
mind brain damage.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:46.760
Take perfectly normal people.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:49.640
You test a hundred of them
on their face recognition

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:52.700
abilities and what you find
is that those abilities

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:54.890
are widely varying.

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:58.610
And some people are just
awful, like the bottom two

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:01.880
people in your group of
100, those bottom two people

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:05.100
routinely fail to
recognize family members.

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:07.460
I mean, that's really bad.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:12.140
And the top 2% of people are
so good that they routinely

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:14.000
hide it from others
because other people

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:15.770
would find it creepy.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:17.570
SARAH HANSEN: So I
live with someone who

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:20.750
on a scale of 1 to
10 probably a three.

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:23.540
I would say I'm about a six.

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:25.580
And where would
you put yourself?

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:27.860
NANCY KANWISHER: Oh, I'm
about a two or a three.

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:29.480
I'm really bad.

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:32.000
I have to ask my partner
when we're watching a movie,

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:33.320
is that the protagonist?

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:35.710
Is that the other one?

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:36.870
Oh, I'm bad.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:37.370
Yeah.

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:38.120
SARAH HANSEN: Wow.

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:42.470
While each individual
part of the brain

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:46.520
is uniquely important, one
big takeaway Nancy explained

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:50.810
is that every mental function
requires many brain regions.

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:53.510
And understanding that is
key to understanding how

00:06:53.510 --> 00:06:56.157
the brain operates as a whole.

00:06:56.157 --> 00:06:58.740
NANCY KANWISHER: Nothing lives
in just one piece of the brain.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:02.090
So even when I carry on about
how some parts of the brain

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:04.850
do very specific
things, that doesn't

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:07.730
mean that they do that
very specific thing alone.

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:11.300
Every mental function requires
many different brain regions.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:13.670
The part that's involved
in face recognition

00:07:13.670 --> 00:07:16.820
could not do its task
without getting input

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:21.080
from primary visual cortex and
other basic visual regions.

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:24.800
And there would be no
use of face recognition

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:26.390
if it didn't send
its information

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:28.970
to other parts of the
brain that know things

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:31.550
like your Rolodex of people.

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:33.150
You have that
someplace in the brain.

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:35.150
It's not very well
understood, but someplace you

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:38.390
know all the facts you know
about the people you know.

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:40.730
And so each of
those brain regions

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:44.840
acts in concert with lots
of other brain regions.

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:47.480
SARAH HANSEN: It's one thing to
understand how the brain works

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:53.090
in the abstract, but in her
course, 9.13 The Human Brain,

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:56.990
Nancy connects neuroscience with
something much more personal.

00:07:56.990 --> 00:08:00.800
She begins the semester with
a story, a personal anecdote

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:03.920
she uses to paint a picture
of all the various topics she

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:06.555
seeks to introduce in her class.

00:08:06.555 --> 00:08:08.180
NANCY KANWISHER: It's
widely recognized

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:11.690
that people like stories and
people resonate to stories.

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:15.200
There's a whole developing
meme and science

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:17.720
communication about how you're
supposed to tell stories.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:19.010
And for a long time
it's, like, yeah,

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:20.480
I know I supposed
to tell a story,

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:22.880
but I just have my
lectures and I don't know--

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:26.180
I don't know what
story to tell or I--

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:30.050
and then I realized oh my
god, I do have a story.

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:33.380
And I was psyched to
realize that, actually,

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:36.860
that story echoes,
to an eerie degree,

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:39.770
all of the themes of the course.

00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:41.539
And it's the story
of a friend of mine

00:08:41.539 --> 00:08:45.980
who had a brain tumor, which was
revealed very dramatically when

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:50.750
he collapsed on the floor
at my house unconscious.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:54.890
Horrifying, terrifying, but
it pulls people in right away.

00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:57.420
He's fine now, to cut to
the chase, totally fine,

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:01.340
but in between, there was quite
a scary and fascinating set

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:04.670
of developments in which
he had a benign tumor that

00:09:04.670 --> 00:09:08.210
was pressing against
a region of his brain,

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:10.910
that my lab had
studied, that's involved

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:12.950
in knowing where you are.

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:15.740
And I knew this guy pretty
well, and so I had actually

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:18.140
been noticing for
a few years that he

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:20.598
seemed to have lost his
ability to navigate around

00:09:20.598 --> 00:09:21.140
in the world.

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:25.340
And it scared me and worried
me, but I just didn't even

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:27.750
make myself think about it.

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:32.900
And so I should have known
what was up with this guy.

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:34.490
I had all the data
in front of me

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:37.880
and I somehow didn't, until
he collapsed on the floor

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:39.410
and it kind of
all came together.

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:42.620
And I thought, oh my god,
what's going on with his brain?

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:47.150
And further, eerily, we
had actually scanned him

00:09:47.150 --> 00:09:49.220
in one of the standard
experiments in my lab

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:54.770
many years before, and
so when he called me,

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:59.480
after he collapsed, and he
recovered a minute or two later

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:01.220
and spent the day in
the ER while they're

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:03.262
trying to figure out what
was happening with him,

00:10:03.262 --> 00:10:05.970
and all day long the
ER docs are saying,

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:07.490
oh, this is a heart
thing, this is

00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:09.530
whatever, we'll figure it out.

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:12.380
And after test, after
test, after test,

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:15.380
that night he calls me and he
says, get over here right now.

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:17.570
They found something
in my brain.

00:10:17.570 --> 00:10:18.890
So I was just horrified.

00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:22.280
And as I'm driving
over to the ER,

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:24.630
I called one of my
lab techs and I said,

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:27.380
hey, we scanned
this guy years ago.

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:29.180
See if you can dig
around in the files

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:31.940
and find the anatomical
scans we would have made

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:34.460
when we did that brain study.

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:38.077
And so by the time I
got there, the MRI tech

00:10:38.077 --> 00:10:40.160
did something they're
probably not supposed to do,

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:42.680
but they showed him and
me pictures of his brain

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:44.995
with this huge thing,
the size of a lime,

00:10:44.995 --> 00:10:46.370
right in the middle
of his brain.

00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:48.410
It was just horrifying.

00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:50.600
And then I was able
to look at the images

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:54.450
that my lab had collected
quite a few years before.

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:57.740
And we could see it,
even many years before.

00:10:57.740 --> 00:11:01.310
It was much smaller,
but clearly visible.

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:03.680
And that was very
important, clinically,

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:07.940
because it showed that this
was a very slow growing tumor.

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:09.530
And that was good news.

00:11:09.530 --> 00:11:14.250
And very diagnostic about
what kind of tumor it was.

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:16.110
SARAH HANSEN: As scary
as this experience

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:20.430
was, not just for Nancy,
but also for her friend,

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:23.250
it led to some
important discoveries.

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:25.320
Nancy was able to
test and receive

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:29.340
results in real time about how
her friend's navigation ability

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:31.680
was impacted by the tumor.

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:34.800
Because of this, she was able
to confirm her own research

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:37.860
about how his ability to
navigate the world around him

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:40.410
was impacted by the
tumor pressing down

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:43.618
on a specific
region of his brain.

00:11:43.618 --> 00:11:45.160
NANCY KANWISHER: In
the whole process

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:48.040
of watching my friend
go through this,

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:50.680
I tested him on
a bunch of tasks.

00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:51.880
He was totally game.

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:57.670
It's like try drawing a
floor plan of your apartment,

00:11:57.670 --> 00:12:00.620
and he draws this total mess.

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:02.380
I mean, it just--

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:03.650
I've been in his apartment.

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:04.900
I know what the floor plan is.

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:08.260
And what he drew had just
no relation whatsoever.

00:12:08.260 --> 00:12:11.860
And then I said, OK,
try drawing a floor plan

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:13.930
of the upstairs of my house.

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:17.060
We were in the downstairs
of my house right then.

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:17.960
Couldn't do it.

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:19.460
And then I thought,
well, maybe it's

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:21.852
just complex multi-part
images, things

00:12:21.852 --> 00:12:23.060
that are hard to reconstruct.

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:27.620
So I said, OK, try drawing
a sketch of a bicycle.

00:12:27.620 --> 00:12:29.160
So he draws a bicycle.

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:31.700
It was not beautiful, but
it was clearly recognizable.

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:34.610
It had all the right parts
in the right positions.

00:12:34.610 --> 00:12:37.250
And that's a very
low tech simple

00:12:37.250 --> 00:12:39.920
example of one of the
main methods in my field,

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:42.200
is studying people
with brain damage

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:46.970
and trying to analyze exactly
what is it that's messed up

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:48.530
and what is it that's preserved.

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:50.900
And that shows you
this implausible split.

00:12:50.900 --> 00:12:53.150
Why would you be
able to remember

00:12:53.150 --> 00:12:57.020
the visual appearance of places
but not the visual appearance

00:12:57.020 --> 00:12:57.950
of objects?

00:12:57.950 --> 00:13:00.680
But that's exactly what,
actually, my own research

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:03.623
predicted.

00:13:03.623 --> 00:13:05.790
SARAH HANSEN: I asked Nancy
how her students respond

00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:07.410
to hearing this story.

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:11.520
She told me that students
respond by asking questions.

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:14.820
This idea of investigating,
questioning, and really holding

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:17.580
on to curiosity
is exactly what's

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:20.352
at the heart of
Nancy's teaching.

00:13:20.352 --> 00:13:22.810
NANCY KANWISHER: They proceed
to ask all the questions that

00:13:22.810 --> 00:13:24.908
are the basic
questions that we're

00:13:24.908 --> 00:13:26.200
going to explore in the course.

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:28.630
I just tell them the story
and then they say OK,

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:30.100
did it get better?

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:31.690
How long did you know?

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:33.233
What other symptoms did he have?

00:13:33.233 --> 00:13:34.900
Did he have this
particular other thing?

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:37.690
Did it affect other
things besides scenes?

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:40.210
How would you study
it besides MRI?

00:13:40.210 --> 00:13:41.650
Would you-- on and on and on.

00:13:41.650 --> 00:13:44.392
But it's like every
single question they ask,

00:13:44.392 --> 00:13:45.850
these are just the
questions we ask

00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:48.743
in my field, which is one of the
things I love about this field,

00:13:48.743 --> 00:13:50.410
is that it's not like
quantum mechanics.

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:52.810
You don't need to study for
years and years and years

00:13:52.810 --> 00:13:54.700
before you even have
the mental space where

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:56.530
you can frame the questions.

00:13:56.530 --> 00:13:58.630
All of the questions
of interest in my field

00:13:58.630 --> 00:14:00.700
are questions that any
normal person can ask

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:02.630
with no background whatsoever.

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:05.570
That's the cool thing about
the brain, is we have one

00:14:05.570 --> 00:14:08.800
and we get to use it and we
get to watch it in action.

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:11.350
And we have already been
wondering all these questions,

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:13.930
whether we realize it or not.

00:14:13.930 --> 00:14:16.360
I mean, one of my favorite
things to point out

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:18.940
is that lots of
questions that people

00:14:18.940 --> 00:14:22.600
have been wondering about
anyway, like how solid

00:14:22.600 --> 00:14:23.950
is human knowledge.

00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:25.180
We think we know this stuff.

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:26.500
Do we really know it?

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:28.000
Are we fooling ourselves?

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:30.250
Those are questions
that can be asked

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:33.160
in lots of different ways, but
one of the ways of asking them

00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:36.370
is to look at brain development
and where the structure

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:37.900
of the brain comes from.

00:14:37.900 --> 00:14:41.410
The kinds of questions that
you can ask and sometimes

00:14:41.410 --> 00:14:45.460
even answer in my field are
questions that lots of people

00:14:45.460 --> 00:14:47.650
wonder about.

00:14:47.650 --> 00:14:50.050
SARAH HANSEN: This was a
particularly powerful part

00:14:50.050 --> 00:14:52.090
of the conversation for me.

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:55.660
The idea that this kind
of study is accessible,

00:14:55.660 --> 00:14:57.130
that's not something
you typically

00:14:57.130 --> 00:15:00.550
infer when you hear
cognitive neuroscience.

00:15:00.550 --> 00:15:03.340
Nancy Kanwisher
wants to change that.

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:05.890
Part of her mission is
to allow everyone access

00:15:05.890 --> 00:15:08.300
to the knowledge
generated by the field.

00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:10.300
NANCY KANWISHER: The
public pays for the science

00:15:10.300 --> 00:15:12.160
we do in the lab,
and so I think it's

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:16.180
a moral obligation of
scientists to give back.

00:15:16.180 --> 00:15:19.420
And I think I'm in the lucky
position that the stuff I do

00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:22.070
is easily shareable with people.

00:15:22.070 --> 00:15:25.820
And so I think it would be
just not OK not to share it.

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:28.210
And so I try to share
it whenever I can.

00:15:28.210 --> 00:15:30.010
But it's also fun.

00:15:30.010 --> 00:15:32.620
It's really fun to get
an idea across and see

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:34.160
somebody resonate to it.

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:36.127
So I think an
educational goal that I

00:15:36.127 --> 00:15:37.960
don't think I've quite
figured out how to do

00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:41.990
but that I aspire towards
is to give people a push

00:15:41.990 --> 00:15:45.460
and encourage them to find
a few things because that

00:15:45.460 --> 00:15:48.730
will teach them that they can
then go find more on their own.

00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:51.580
I would love it if
some of my research

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:54.010
would actually
help people, but it

00:15:54.010 --> 00:15:56.830
would be a lie if I told
you that that's why I do it

00:15:56.830 --> 00:15:59.440
or that we have already been
able to help a lot of people.

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:00.850
That's just not true.

00:16:00.850 --> 00:16:03.070
The real thing that
motivates me is I

00:16:03.070 --> 00:16:07.480
just think it's the grandest
scientific quest of all time

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:09.310
to understand the human mind.

00:16:09.310 --> 00:16:11.510
And looking at a brain is
a great way to do that.

00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:13.240
So that's really
what I'm in it for,

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:19.000
is just the pure fascination
of understanding our own minds.

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:22.390
To me, it's like all
about sharing things

00:16:22.390 --> 00:16:25.570
that I think are cool
because, actually, my teaching

00:16:25.570 --> 00:16:29.070
goal is to blow people's minds.

00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:32.015
SARAH HANSEN: Blowing people's
minds by teaching about them.

00:16:32.015 --> 00:16:33.390
It's one of the
things that makes

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:36.870
Nancy's course so compelling.

00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:39.210
And by the way, I
asked Nancy if there

00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:40.920
were insights about
teaching that she

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:43.253
wished our listeners
would share with her.

00:16:43.253 --> 00:16:45.420
NANCY KANWISHER: Well, there
are all kinds of topics

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:48.120
that I haven't figured out how
to lecture on, that I just have

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:50.160
a blind spot on, like memory.

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:52.080
This is a big part of the field.

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:53.820
There's really
beautiful work on it.

00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:55.680
Lots of people study it.

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:59.100
But I have just a
hard time motivating

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:01.530
what the big questions are,
and so I don't actually

00:17:01.530 --> 00:17:02.370
lecture on memory.

00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:03.690
And I'm really
embarrassed about that,

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:05.107
but I just haven't
figured out how

00:17:05.107 --> 00:17:06.900
to give a good lecture on it.

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:08.670
That's one.

00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:09.222
Emotions.

00:17:09.222 --> 00:17:11.430
I've never figured out how
to lecture about emotions.

00:17:11.430 --> 00:17:13.020
And of course, everybody
cares about them

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:14.603
and they're really
important and there

00:17:14.603 --> 00:17:18.900
are neural stories to be told
and experiments to read about.

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:23.150
And I am just not very
good on that topic.

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:25.020
SARAH HANSEN: If you
have insights to share,

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:27.920
please get in touch with us
at the link in our show notes.

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:30.050
And when you do,
you'll be joining Nancy

00:17:30.050 --> 00:17:35.470
in making cognitive neuroscience
more accessible to all of us.

00:17:35.470 --> 00:17:38.350
If you're interested in learning
from her open and free teaching

00:17:38.350 --> 00:17:41.530
materials or remixing
them in your own teaching,

00:17:41.530 --> 00:17:45.700
you can find them on our
MIT OpenCourseWare website.

00:17:45.700 --> 00:17:47.800
Additionally, you
can find Nancy's work

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:51.246
at the McGovern Institute by
visiting McGovern.mit.edu.

00:17:53.922 --> 00:17:56.030
Thank you so much for listening.

00:17:56.030 --> 00:17:59.960
Until next time, signing off
from Cambridge, Massachusetts,

00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:05.320
I'm your host, Sarah Hansen
from MIT OpenCourseWare.

